  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Authentication Endpoint**\n\nI\'ve just pinned down the specifics for the Daidai-panel open API token endpoint: it\'s a POST request to `/api/v1/open-api/token` with either `{ appKey: \"...\", appSecret: \"...\" }` or `{ AppKey: \"...\", AppSecret: \"...\" }` in the body. A successful response includes a 200 code and a `data` object containing the generated token.\n\n\n**Refining API Endpoint Documentation**\n\nI\'ve outlined how the generated token will be passed, using an Authorization Bearer header. I\'ve also defined the `/api/v1/envs` endpoint for environment management, including GET, POST, and PUT methods for querying, creating, and updating environments respectively, along with a search parameter. I\'m now reviewing the user instructions.\n\n\n**Implementing the Question Phase**\n\nI am now structuring the initial interaction when planning mode is activated. I\'m focusing on the first phase, which involves asking clarifying questions. I\'m preparing to call the `ask_question` tool with 2-4 multiple-choice questions, adhering to the specified format: plain text, concise option labels (under 10 words), and recommended choices. My goal is to extract the necessary information without creating an implementation plan in the first turn.\n\n\n**Clarifying User Requirements**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the question phase structure. I need to nail down the questions. I\'m focusing on clarifying critical points. I am formatting multiple-choice options in the user\'s direct response, without \'Other\' options, as buttons, and prefixed recommended responses, as requested. The application format and deployment approach needs further clarification.\n\n\n**Formulating User Questions**\n\nI\'m finalizing the question set for gathering key details. I\'ve composed three multiple-choice questions focusing on application presentation, clipboard behavior, and environment variable update strategy. Each question has concise options, with recommended choices clearly marked. The goal is to obtain clear user input on critical design choices before proceeding.\n\n\n"
