  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Configuring PWA Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating `vite-plugin-pwa` into `vite.config.ts`. The initial setup involves defining core properties within the configuration, specifically setting the `id` to `/`, `name` to \'\345\221\206\345\221\206\351\235\242\346\235\277\345\211\252\350\264\264\346\235\277\345\220\214\346\255\245\345\212\251\346\211\213\', and `short_name` to \'\345\221\206\345\221\206\345\220\214\346\255\245\'. This is to ensure the PWA has a unique identifier and user-friendly name, as per the `pwa-integration` requirements.\n\n\n**Defining PWA Manifest Details**\n\nI\'ve specified the PWA\'s descriptive elements: a detailed description, color theming, and display mode. I have also set the `start_url` and `scope` to `/`. Next, I\'ve integrated icon definitions in several sizes with the `any` and `maskable` purposes. I\'ve also added dev options and ensured the register type is set to autoUpdate.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Backend Requirements**\n\nI\'m now questioning the necessity of a dedicated backend server. I realized the frontend can potentially directly call the Daidai panel\'s API via `fetch`, assuming the panel is accessible over the network. I\'m exploring scenarios where the user\'s Daidai panel is reached directly via their phone\'s browser or the PWA itself. Considering this, I am trying to determine if a full-stack approach is strictly necessary for this use case.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_190417"
