    thinking: "**Analyzing the Error Source**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on the WebSocket closure error. I\'ve noted the specific error message and am now investigating the environment\'s constraints, specifically the disabled HMR and the instruction to ignore WebSockets. I\'m exploring how these settings might be causing the unexpected closure.\n\n\n**Investigating the WebSocket Connection**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the `server.ts` and `vite.config.ts` files to understand the WebSocket connection attempts. It seems the `middlewareMode: true` setting in Vite might be a key factor. Specifically, I\'m examining how the HMR server is handled in this mode and whether an HTTP server is properly attached for WebSockets. I\'m focusing on why a Vite client would try to connect and subsequently fail with a \"WebSocket closed without opened\" message. I need to understand why the Vite client even initiated the attempt.\n\n\n**Uncovering the HMR Impact**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial realization: the problem might stem directly from `process.env.DISABLE_HMR = \'true\'` in AI Studio, which could explain why Vite injects `/@vite/client` even when HMR is disabled. Now, I\'m verifying if `server.ts` needs Express when HMR is off or when using `http.createServer(app)`. Additionally, I\'m checking environment constraints. I see \"Avoid window.alert/window.open\" in the requirements, and I found multiple `window.alert` calls.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Alert Cause**\n\nMy Eureka moment: I\'ve found the root cause \342\200\223 blatant use of `window.alert` and `window.confirm`! They\'re likely blocked in the iframe environment, leading to the errors. Specifically, the offending lines in `SettingsView.tsx` are now the prime suspect. These MUST be replaced with in-app UI equivalents to resolve the problem! I\'ve also re-examined the WebSocket error in conjunction with the discovered alert calls.\n\n\n"
